BCC_Q({"lv": "910", "y": 2016, "n": 7, "part": "B", "topic": "graph", "title": "Log Mover", "answer": 1, "story": "<p>Leslie the Beaver must drag logs one at a time through the system of\ncanals and stations shown below. The logs must be dragged through canals\nin the direction of the arrows. The number in each arrow is the maximum\ntotal number of logs that can ever be dragged through the corresponding\ncanal.<\/p>\n<div class=\"center\">\n<p><img alt=\"A description of the system of canals follows.\" src=\"data:image/png;base64,\" style=\"width:75%\"/><\/p>\n<\/div>\n<button onclick=\"hideSeek('longdesc9')\" style=\"box-shadow:none\">Hide/Reveal Description of System of Canals<\/button>\n<div id=\"longdesc9\" style=\"display:none\">\n<p>Six stations are arranged like the vertices of a hexagon with two top stations (top-left and top-right), a left station, a right station, and two bottom stations (bottom-left and bottom-right). Eight canals, with directional arrows labelled with a number, point from one station to another as described below.<\/p>\n<ul>\n<li>A canal labelled 4 points from the left station to the top-left station.<\/li>\n<li>A canal labelled 2 points from the top-left station to the top-right station.<\/li>\n<li>A canal labelled 5 points from the top-right station to the right station.<\/li>\n<li>A canal labelled 3 points from the left station to the bottom-left station.<\/li>\n<li>A canal labelled 4 points from the bottom-left station to the bottom-right station.<\/li>\n<li>A canal labelled 2 points from the bottom-right station to the right station.<\/li>\n<li>A canal labelled 2 points from the top-left station to the bottom-left station.<\/li>\n<li>A canal labelled 1 points from the bottom-right station to the top-right station.<\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<hr class=\"longdesc\"/>\n<\/div>", "question": "<p>What is the maximum number of logs that Leslie can drag from the\nstart station to the terminal station?<\/p>\n<ol class=\"mc\">\n<li>4<\/li>\n<li>5<\/li>\n<li>6<\/li>\n<li>7<\/li>\n<\/ol>", "answerHtml": "<p>(B) 5<\/p>", "explanation": "<p>Consider the following solution, where the first number on each arrow\nshows the number of logs that Leslie carries over each canal and the\nsecond number shows its maximum capacity:<\/p>\n<div class=\"center\">\n<p><img alt=\"A description of the solution follows.\" src=\"data:image/png;base64,\" style=\"width:75%\"/><\/p>\n<\/div>\n<button onclick=\"hideSeek('longdesc10')\" style=\"box-shadow:none\">Hide/Reveal Description of Solution Diagram<\/button>\n<div id=\"longdesc10\" style=\"display:none\">\n<p>The numbers on the arrow in each of the canals in the system are as follows.<\/p>\n<ul>\n<li>Left to top-left station: 2, 4<\/li>\n<li>Top-left to top-right station: 2, 2<\/li>\n<li>Top-right to right station: 3, 5<\/li>\n<li>Left station to bottom-left station: 3, 3<\/li>\n<li>Bottom-left to bottom-right station: 3, 3<\/li>\n<li>Bottom-right to right station: 2, 2<\/li>\n<li>Top-left to the bottom-left station: 0, 2<\/li>\n<li>Bottom-right to the top-right station: 1, 1<\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<hr class=\"longdesc\"/>\n<\/div>\n<p>We see that two logs can be dragged along the three top canals. Two\nother logs can be dragged along the bottom canals. A fourth log can be\ndragged along the first two bottom canals, then up a canal and finally\nto the terminal station. This shows that it is possible for Leslie to\ndrag five logs. To see that this is an upper bound, notice the dashed\nline. Each log must be dragged across this line in order to reach the\nterminal station. So at most <span class=\"math inline\">\\(2+1+2=5\\)<\/span> total logs can reach the terminal\nstation.<\/p>", "connections": "<p>The problem of finding a <em>maximum-flow<\/em> in a network is common\nin computer science. A famous theorem (Ford-Fulkerson, 1956) says that\nthe maximum flow (how many logs are dragged over canals) is given by the\ncapacity of the <em>minimum cut<\/em> (dashed line), which if removed\nwould disconnect the <em>source<\/em> (start station) from the\n<em>sink<\/em> (terminal station).<\/p>\n<p>A common example is the Internet, where we want to move as much data\nas possible between routers. Other examples include image processing,\nairline scheduling, network reliability, and distributed computing. In\nmost of these applications, and particularly for the Internet, solving\nthis problem quickly is important since the Internet is composed of\nmillions of such nodes, each with a capacity, so routing through routers\nin this network needs to be done as efficiently as possible.<\/p>"});